<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Akhenaten</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1353–1336 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century-bce">14th century BCE</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Eighteenth Dynasty of Egypt</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Akhenaten was an Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Eighteenth Dynasty</a> who reigned c. 1353–1336 BCE. He promoted the exclusive worship of the sun disc, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aten">Aten</a>, and moved the capital to a new city, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Akhetaten</a> (Amarna). His religious revolution disrupted Egyptian tradition and was reversed after his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/akhenaten.jpg" alt="Akhenaten" width="315" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GD-EG-Caire-Mus%C3%A9e061.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…of the fourteenth century BCE: King Rib-Hadda wrote more than sixty letters to the pharaohs Amenhotep III and <b>Akhenaten</b>, pleading for archers and supplies as Abdi-Ashirta of Amurru and then his son Aziru seized neighbouring towns, cut off the city’s grain supply and turned its own people against…</blockquote><a href="/empire/byblos">Byblos · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Amarna Letters, the diplomatic archive recovered from <b>Akhenaten</b>'s capital, reveal a sophisticated system of international correspondence conducted in Akkadian cuneiform, through which Egypt managed its relationships with Babylon, Mitanni, Assyria, the Hittites, and Alashiya (Cyprus).</blockquote><a href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Hymns to the gods were inscribed in temples and tombs; the Great Hymn to the Aten, from the time of <b>Akhenaten</b>, celebrates the sun as the creator of all life in terms often compared with Psalm 104 of the Hebrew Bible.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-egyptian-religion">Ancient Egyptian religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>The Amarna letters, found in Egypt in 1887, contain the correspondence of the pharaohs Amenhotep III and <b>Akhenaten</b> with the kings of Babylon, Assyria, Mitanni and the Hittites and with the rulers of Canaan, almost all in Akkadian.</blockquote><a href="/language/akkadian">Akkadian · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Old and Middle Egyptian were verb-first languages that expressed grammatical meanings with endings; Late Egyptian, which entered official writing under <b>Akhenaten</b> in the 14th century BCE, used articles, auxiliary verbs and a different word order; demotic and Coptic continued this development.</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-egyptian">Ancient Egyptian · Change &amp; Descendants</a></li><li><blockquote>Their painted pottery, much of it containers for wine and perfumed oil, is found from Sardinia, Sicily and southern Italy to Cyprus, the Levant and Egypt, where it reached <b>Akhenaten</b>’s city at Amarna.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mycenaean-civilisation">Mycenaean civilisation · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
